<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P06_Ex3_Cases_And_Step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>For which of \(n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\) is \(2^n > n^2\)? Enter them as a set, for example <code>{1, 2}</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>In the induction step you show \(2^{k+1} = 2 \times 2^k > 2k^2\), and then you need \(2k^2 \geq (k+1)^2\). What is the smallest positive integer \(k\) for which \(2k^2 \geq (k+1)^2\)?</p>
<div>\(k =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Compare \(2^n\) and \(n^2\):</p>
<table>
<tr><td>\(n\)</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(2^n\)</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>16</td><td>32</td><td>64</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(n^2\)</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>9</td><td>16</td><td>25</td><td>36</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The strict inequality holds for \(n = 1, 5, 6\). At \(n = 2\) and \(n = 4\) the two sides are equal, and at \(n = 3\) it is false. So the answer is \(\{1, 5, 6\}\).</p></li>
<li><p>\(2k^2 - (k+1)^2 = k^2 - 2k - 1 = (k-1)^2 - 2\), which is at least 0 exactly when \((k-1)^2 \geq 2\), that is, for \(k \geq 3\). At \(k = 2\), \(8 < 9\); at \(k = 3\), \(18 \geq 16\). So the smallest \(k\) is 3.</p>
<p>The induction step therefore works from \(k = 3\). But the statement is false at \(n = 3\) and \(n = 4\), so the chain can only start at \(n = 5\), the first true case from which it never breaks. \(P(1)\) is true, but the step cannot carry it on: \(P(2)\) is false, so the step does not work from \(k = 1\).</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
